this video is sponsored by notion the  only constant in life is change as i was  joining the tech industry right after  college that was one of the resounding  pieces of advice i got from my seniors  around me and you know what i think that  being a software engineer really helped  to prepare and teach me to internalize  that i like to think of it as being  light on my feet like not getting stuck  in any one place or thing for too long  so that i can adapt smoothly and easily  and i think that through my adult life  i've gotten used to not just adapting to  big life changes but really embracing  them too it's one of those things though  that's easier said than done it's easy  to say that we'll be ready for changes  but when it comes with discomfort facing  harsh realities and the need to be brave  it's safe to say that change brings  about challenges for us that we must  rise up to and not to mention i have  anxiety so i'm scared of a lot of things  and i'm always worried therapies help me  to address it on a personal level as i  move through times of change but anxiety  is definitely just very common when  you're dealing with the unknown and  you're feeling the undercurrent of  change underneath your feet and in  software we don't really talk about  anxiety so much and instead we focus on  ambiguity as the core of this phenomenon  there's a ton of like articles and  videos and talks about how to mitigate  and manage ambiguity and software but i  really think it just comes down to  learning as much as you can about the  ambiguous thing and then planning for it  so if a project itself is ambiguous or  maybe adding a new technology has  ambiguity then gathering as much  information as possible to then make an  informed plan  on what steps to take next is one way to  make everything go smoother in fact  that's something that i even brought to  my work as a content creator because my  work if anything is super ambiguous  literally the work that i do is just so  different from anything i've done before  and there's no paved path in front of me  and a lot of times i can't exactly ask  family or friends for like career advice  because my family and friends are also  not youtubers it's just the work i do is  just so different from anything i've  ever done before and there isn't like a  paved career path in front of me and so  i have to kind of figure out a lot of  this stuff on my own as i do it because  everyone does content differently they  create different types of videos and  posts to a different type of audience in  a different way that works for the  creator themselves it's still really  easy to lose myself and like the great  mess of social media and get lost in the  ambiguity but luckily software  engineering has taught me some really  valuable lessons and how to work through  this so what does that actually look  like in practice well it looks like  ideating on video ideas with my team on  what would be interesting to make and  also watch on youtube it also means  creating weekly plans for how to bring  that video to life and scheduling those  in the future and then tracking the  progress of those videos while gathering  feedback reducing ambiguity through  planning is something that i used in my  personal life this year as well as my  husband and i embarked on a kitchen  renovation on our new home  we started with what we thought we might  want the end product to be with  brainstorm docs which then resulted in  putting together a detailed pdf for a  contractor to share with him our vision  for a future kitchen and then throughout  our renovation keeping track of our own  to do's like ordering parts and making  calls to subcontractors for things like  flooring and countertops we even tracked  our budget too to make sure that we were  spending our money in the right places  and we could keep track of our spending  all of these things ideating  collaborating planning and tracking all  help me to crystallize the goals that i  have in both my personal and work life  to help me work through the mountain of  ambiguity that exists because again  change is the only constant and these  days i want change i want to engage with  the ever-changing tides of life as i  embark on new challenges and learn new  things i mean after all it's going  through these changes that are gonna  help us to learn some of the most  valuable life lessons rather than the  end result itself staying curious and  moving through life seasons is i think  one of the best ways to feel like you're  living  thank you so much for watching this  video i really hope you liked it i just  want to say thank you again so much to  notion for partnering with me on this  video and helping me to move through all  the life's changes easily and  thoughtfully notion is an amazing  collaboration tool that helps you dream  plan work and build with others the way  you want  genuinely notion is just one of my  favorite tools it's really helped  enhance everything that i need about  note-taking planning i dating  collaborating i actually did a notion  setup tour earlier this year and if you  haven't checked that out i'll link it  right here and if you're interested in  also inviting notion into your life get  started for free and create a new  teamwork space and or invite a team  member to a notion page using my link  don't forget to subscribe as always and  i'll see you next time bye  [Music]
 

